= Self-organization — Technical =

== Decentralized order ==
A self-organizing system typically produces macrostructure from repeated local transition rules or interaction dynamics:

<pre>
local state + neighborhood/environment
→ update
→ repeated interaction
→ macro-pattern
</pre>

== Mechanisms ==
Mechanisms can include:

* positive and negative feedback;
* symmetry breaking;
* reaction–diffusion;
* local alignment;
* threshold dynamics;
* distributed adaptation;
* attractor formation.

No one mechanism defines all self-organization.

== External constraints ==
Boundary conditions and external forcing can shape the space of possible self-organized states without specifying the exact resulting pattern.

